---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
interface Snapshot { sourceRevision: string; ashRevision: string; milestone: string; lessonCount: number; fetchedAt: string; }
let snapshot: Snapshot | undefined;
try { snapshot = JSON.parse(readFileSync(new URL('../../../public/study/latest/snapshot.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8')); } catch { /* development before first build */ }
---
<Article title="Take time to understand the program" description="Read the latest Ash study material here: connected lessons, executable examples, and worked cases." section="Study">
  <p>Ash-doc is the study companion to this website. It remains a separate project, while each site build pulls its latest documentation and renders its indexed learning material here.</p>
  {snapshot ? <>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Latest development snapshot · {snapshot.milestone}</strong><p>This is working documentation, not a published book edition. These {snapshot.lessonCount} lessons target Ash <a href={`https://github.com/dikini/ash-lang/tree/${snapshot.ashRevision}`}>{snapshot.ashRevision.slice(0, 7)}</a>. Use the study material’s own setup and compiler target.</p><p>Documentation source: <a href={`https://github.com/dikini/ash-doc/tree/${snapshot.sourceRevision}`}>{snapshot.sourceRevision.slice(0, 7)}</a>. Retrieved <time datetime={snapshot.fetchedAt}>{new Date(snapshot.fetchedAt).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { timeZone: 'UTC', day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' })}</time>.</p></div>
    <p><a class="button primary" href="/study/latest/">Read the study material →</a></p>
    <h2>Begin at the beginning</h2><p><a href="/study/latest/setup.html">Prepare the study edition’s Ash tool</a>, then begin with <a href="/study/latest/learn-introduction.html">Learn Ash</a>. The lessons develop values, functions, modules, data, recursion, and abstraction.</p>
    <h2>Continue with a deeper topic</h2><ul><li><a href="/study/latest/libraries-introduction.html">Library abstractions</a>: interfaces, evidence, type constructors, and nominal wrappers.</li><li><a href="/study/latest/effects-introduction.html">Effects and scoped resources</a>: requests, handlers, state, cleanup, and composition.</li><li><a href="/study/latest/cases-introduction.html">Worked cases</a>: representations, algorithms, invariants, and effectful programs.</li></ul>
    <h2>One source, with its evidence intact</h2><p>The site reuses ash-doc’s HTML renderer, reading order, examples, notes, and figures. Building the website checks source and rendered-content agreement; it does not rerun the documentation’s compiler examples or declare them newly verified.</p><p>EPUB and PDF books remain owned by ash-doc. They are not generated or offered as published editions by this website build. The <a href="/study/latest/snapshot.json">snapshot manifest</a> records the exact source and compiler target.</p>
  </> : <div class="callout"><strong>Study material has not been prepared locally.</strong><p>Run <code>npm run build</code> to fetch and render the latest documentation. You can also browse the <a href="https://github.com/dikini/ash-doc">ash-doc repository</a>.</p></div>}
  <p>For a quick introduction, try the <a href="/learn/tour/">website tour</a>. For implemented capabilities at the website’s own checkpoint, read <a href="/status/">language status</a>.</p>
</Article>
